;; Seatbelt profile for the gate battery. Applied by scripts/local-gates.sh to
;; the battery invocation ONLY, never to the runner's own fetch or status post.
;;
;; WHAT THIS REPLACES. GitHub Actions gave a fork pull request an isolated
;; runner and a read-only token. A local runner has no such boundary: the
;; battery executes files from the checkout as the machine owner, with the
;; keychain, the ssh keys and a GitHub token carrying repo, workflow, gist and
;; user scopes all readable. Nothing local reproduces a container, and this
;; profile does not pretend to. It removes the two things a hostile test file
;; would actually want: the credentials, and a way to send them anywhere.
;;
;; ALLOW-DEFAULT WITH A DENYLIST, deliberately, and the trade is stated rather
;; than buried. A deny-default profile is stronger and would need every
;; toolchain path the two batteries read enumerated, which is a long tail that
;; breaks on the next dependency and gets switched off the first time it fires
;; on honest work. A gate that fires on normal work gets disabled, and a
;; disabled gate protects nothing. So this denies exactly the assets the parity
;; audit named, and an unlisted path stays readable. That is a real gap and it
;; is written into docs/KNOWN-LIMITS.md rather than left implicit.
;;
;; SECOND KNOWN LIMIT: sandbox-exec is deprecated by Apple. It is present and
;; functional on this machine and has been for many releases, but it is not a
;; guarantee for the future. If it is ever removed, the honest response is to
;; report the loss as a regression in the parity ledger, not to quietly drop
;; the wrapper and keep the old score.
(version 1)
(allow default)

;; No network. A test that needs the network is a test that can exfiltrate, and
;; neither battery legitimately reaches out: BrotherSBE's PR-verify suite is
;; explicitly the canned, offline one ("every GitHub API call is routed through
;; a fake fetch, so it needs no network and no token"), and the citation check
;; states on every run that it opens no network connection.
(deny network*)

;; The credentials themselves. These are the paths a leaked-token forger or a
;; hostile fixture would go for, and none of them is anything a test of this
;; code has any business reading.
(deny file-read*
  (subpath (param "HOME_SSH"))
  (subpath (param "HOME_GH"))
  (subpath (param "HOME_KEYCHAINS"))
  (subpath (param "HOME_AWS"))
  (subpath (param "VAULT")))
